1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,900
YAEC answers one question: can YOUR station work the DX via the Moon, when, and how well?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,700
In this video: an example station in Capri (JN70CN) and a DX in Hawaii (BL20BB), 144 MHz.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,700
Where the Moon is right now, and whether your antenna can actually reach it.

4
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:15,600
Locators up to 14 characters, UTC, local and DX clocks, and a countdown to the next common window.

5
00:00:16,600 --> 00:00:20,700
A table for any period, coloured by what your antenna can really point at.

6
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:24,600
Common windows with the DX are marked; the whole table exports to CSV for Excel.

7
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:29,700
The sky from your QTH, coloured by sky noise at your frequency.

8
00:00:29,800 --> 00:00:33,600
With your antenna limits, the DX horizon, radio sources, the Sun and the Moon path.

9
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:38,700
The real terrain around you from SRTM1 elevation data, with Earth curvature and refraction.

10
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:42,600
Moonrise and moonset above the hills, named obstacles, and the minutes of Moon you lose.

11
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:47,700
Up to 61 days of degradation (Dgrd) and Moon distance, with perigee, apogee and new Moon.

12
00:00:47,800 --> 00:00:51,600
The three best days are picked counting only the hours your antenna can really point.

13
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,700
Degradation at your working frequency, with the real noise of your own station.

14
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:00,600
The sky is averaged over your antenna beam, and compared with the usual WSJT-X value.

15
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:05,700
A complete link budget, band by band: EIRP, path loss and S/N in both directions.

16
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:09,600
Cables, preamp, transverter and ground gain; JT65 and Q65 modes; echo level, delay and Doppler.

17
00:01:10,600 --> 00:01:14,700
The same budget for dishes on 432, 902 and 1296 MHz, prime focus or offset.

18
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:18,600
Gain and beamwidth from published formulas, circular polarization on 23 cm.

19
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:23,700
Measured solar flux from the RSTN network (NOAA), downloaded for you.

20
00:01:23,800 --> 00:01:27,600
It estimates how much the Sun raises your receiver noise near new Moon.

21
00:01:28,600 --> 00:01:32,700
The French GRAVES radar on 143.050 MHz as a test bench for your receive chain.

22
00:01:32,800 --> 00:01:36,600
When the Moon is in its beam and inside your antenna limits, with Doppler and echo delay.

23
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:41,700
Active stations from the N0UK EME chat that share a window with you on the chosen day.

24
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:45,600
Ranked 0 to 100 from the expected S/N in both directions. Idea by Matteo IW5DHN.

25
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:50,700
The Moon elevation seen from every place on Earth, at the chosen moment.

26
00:01:50,800 --> 00:01:54,600
Click anywhere on the map to make that point your DX station.

27
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:59,700
Every formula can be checked step by step with the values substituted, and exported to Excel.

28
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:03,600
Moon positions accurate to about 0.01° (Meeus, ELP-2000/82), all computed on your PC.

29
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:11,400
Download it, try it on your next EME window, and tell us what you find.
